{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== HARNESS RUN 4 (30724309686, main @ 216ceb830): 42 passed, 7 failed. Was 29/19. ===\n\nTHE UNBUNDLING WORKED, AND THE PREDICTION HELD EXACTLY. Run 3's 19 failures were diagnosed as\n13 false reds (mutations that round-tripped but tripped a bundled idempotence assertion) plus 5-6\ngenuine findings. Change 1188 hoisted idempotence into its own cell and fixed the plan-entry\nmatching. Result: **13 cells flipped to green and nothing regressed** — every remaining failure is\nnow a real, attributable finding rather than collateral. That is the whole value of \"an assertion\nthat can fail for a reason unrelated to the cell's subject belongs in its own cell\".\n\n*** PAGINATION NOW PASSES, AND IT PROVED SOMETHING REAL. ***\n`test_the_inbound_snapshot_survives_multi_page_pagination` PASSED, reporting (via `-rA`):\n    server-echoed maxResults=1000; reconciler page_size=100; effective=100;\n    seeding 201 issues to force 3 pages\n    indexed 201 of 201 seeded issues\n    recovered 201 of 201 seeded issues\nSo the effective page size is OBSERVED (read from the server's own echoed `maxResults`, not\nassumed), 2*100+1 issues were seeded to force THREE pages, and the inbound fetch recovered ALL of\nthem. That is live evidence against the truncation class that shipped three times in this epic\n(1105 `get_parent_map`, 9263 `get_issuelinks_map`/`get_comment_map`, deac `fetcher._iter_pages`),\nand it is exactly the AC the plan wrote.\nRun 3's \"recovered only 0 of 201 — 201 silently LOST\" was my filter, not the fetch, as recorded in\nthe previous comment. Confirmed by this run.\n\n*** THE 7 REMAINING FAILURES — all genuine, and one has CHANGED MEANING ***\n 1. `test_a_repeat_pass_over_a_converged_pair_plans_nothing` — **NOW A REAL PRODUCT FINDING.** The\n    standalone cell asserts the write LANDED on the instance AND waited until a JQL search\n    reflects it, and only then re-plans. It still reports a planned outbound update. Index lag is\n    excluded by construction, so this is genuine churn: the differ believes there is work after\n    convergence. This is the single most interesting result in the run and deserves its own\n    ticket — it is the unconvergeable-churn class J4's anti-churn oracle exists to prevent.\n 2 & 3. `inbound_mutation_round_trips[04-transition-status]` AND\n    `outbound_mutation_round_trips[04-transition-status]` — BOTH directions of status fail. Two\n    cells, most likely ONE cause. Check first whether the harness project's workflow even offers\n    an \"In Progress\" transition from the issue's current state: `transition_issue_by_name` raises\n    with the available list, and if the pass swallows that the symptom looks like a bridge defect.\n    Do not assume the bridge before reading that list.\n 4. `inbound_mutation_round_trips[02-edit-summary]` — the new summary does not reach the local\n    ticket, while `03-edit-description` DOES on the same path. Title-specific; do not generalise.\n 5. `test_inbound_link_round_trips` — the Jira link does not surface as a local dep. Note the\n    OUTBOUND direction (`test_outbound_link_round_trips`) PASSES, so links work one way.\n 6. `test_a_deleted_dc_issue_is_planned_as_an_inbound_probe` — deleting the issue plans no probe.\n 7. `test_the_inbound_assignee_mints_a_jira_family_identity` — `.assignee` is the RAW DC username\n    `'admin'` rather than a minted rebar identity id. The most substantive of the field findings:\n    it means the inbound assignee is stored verbatim rather than resolved through the identity\n    registry, which is the territory bug 5f48 was circling.\n\nWHAT IS PROVEN AND STABLE ACROSS FOUR RUNS: the epic's headline AC in BOTH directions, isolation\nof both repos, a complete count-matched scrubbed store copy, zero deletions and zero outbound\nupdates over it, and now multi-page pagination. 42 of 49 cells green.\n\nNEXT: triage items 2&3 together (one likely cause), then 7, then 1 (which probably becomes its own\nbug ticket rather than a J11 fix), then 4, 5, 6."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785631180925948001,"uuid":"b57d3d7a-6dd3-4584-bd33-6c777498f495"}